WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.669
Be honest. Does $19.99 feel a lot cheaper than $20?

2
00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:06.714
It's one penny. One. Penny.

3
00:00:07.664 --> 00:00:11.163
Your brain reads prices left to right, and it gets a little lazy.

4
00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:15.599
It sees the one, decides it's nineteen-something, and stops paying attention.

5
00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:18.093
Marketers call this the left-digit effect.

6
00:00:19.043 --> 00:00:22.760
Pricing just below a round number is called odd-even pricing.

7
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.540
That's why Carmex, gas stations, and almost

8
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.981
every store you know love the number nine.

9
00:00:28.931 --> 00:00:30.071
But there's a flip side.

10
00:00:30.351 --> 00:00:35.553
Luxury brands often skip the nines and use round numbers, like $200 flat.

11
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:38.538
Round prices feel premium, not bargain bin.

12
00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:41.443
Next time you shop, play detective.

13
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:45.157
Count the nines, and ask what the price is trying to make you feel.
